<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Srivijaya</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Srivijaya Empire, Srivijayan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Srivijaya was a maritime empire based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> from the seventh century to around the thirteenth century. It controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda Strait</a> and was a major centre of Buddhist learning. It declined after attacks by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola Empire</a> and the rise of Javanese rivals.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/srivijaya.jpg" alt="Srivijaya" width="320" height="326" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gunawan Kartapranata · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Srivijaya_Empire.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The earliest reference to Srivijaya dates from the 7th century. The Tang dynasty Chinese monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yijing">Yijing</a> wrote that he visited it for six months in 671. The earliest known inscription naming it is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kedukan-bukit-inscription">Kedukan Bukit inscription</a>, found near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a> and dated 605 Saka, which corresponds to 682 or 683. It tells of Dapunta Hyang Sri Jayanasa, who set out on a sacred siddhayatra journey with 20,000 troops from Minanga Tamwan to Jambi and Palembang and founded a kadatuan, a kingdom or polity.</p><p>The name itself is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, meaning something like glorious or shining victory. Early 20th-century scholars thought it was a king's name, until the French historian George Cœdès postulated the empire's existence in 1918. Its history had been forgotten, and Indonesians, even around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a>, had not heard of it until the 1920s, when Cœdès published his findings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and Malay newspapers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The kingdom first had to consolidate its position in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, which consisted of several quasi-independent polities ruled by local chieftains, and many of its forces were sea peoples, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orang-laut">orang laut</a>. During the 7th century Langkasuka on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a> became part of it, and Pan Pan and Tambralinga came under its influence. In the second half of the 8th century its mandala seems to have been ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sailendra-dynasty">Sailendra dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-java">Central Java</a>, although it is not known whether the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> or Srivijaya became subordinate to it. Between the late 7th and early 11th century it was a hegemon in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> and a centre for the expansion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>.</p><p>Rajendra <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a>, the Chola king from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, launched naval raids on its ports in 1025. His fleet sailed with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> winds and took Srivijaya by surprise, first ransacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a> and then moving on to other ports, including Kadaram. An inscription of Rajendra says he captured its king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangrama-vijayatunggavarman">Sangrama Vijayatunggavarman</a>, and plundered treasures such as the jewelled war gate. The kingdom may have disintegrated after 1025, though Chinese sources continued to refer to a polity called Sanfoqi for some centuries, which some historians identify with Jambi after that date.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>In its formation the empire had three zones: the capital region around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musi-river">Musi River</a> basin as a source of valuable goods, and rival river-mouth areas, such as the Batang Hari in Jambi, held under its power by raids and conquests. Its main foreign aim was a lucrative trade agreement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, kept through tribute missions from the Tang to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a>, and it controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> straits between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and China. Historians debate whether it was chiefly a sea power or a land-based polity.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Srivijaya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cham merchants settled in Sulu from the 10th to the 13th centuries, where they were called Orang Dampuan; Champa traded with Ma-i and Butuan in the Philippines and with <b>Srivijaya</b> and later Majapahit.</blockquote><a href="/empire/champa">Champa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Water Chenla, on the Mekong Delta, had the river's wealth but was more exposed; late in the century it faced war from Javanese pirates who took the delta and then Chenla itself, and it came to depend on the Shailendra dynasty of Java and <b>Srivijaya</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chenla">Chenla · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The shift of trade to Sumatra, the rise of <b>Srivijaya</b> and China's taking of the routes through Southeast Asia brought economic instability to the south and forced politics and the economy northward.</blockquote><a href="/empire/funan">Funan · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Strait of Malacca had been dominated by <b>Srivijaya</b>, centred on Palembang, until the Cholas weakened it in the 11th century, and by the end of the 13th by the Javanese Singhasari and Majapahit.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malacca-sultanate">Malacca Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Dharanindra (r. 780–800), hailed in the Kelurak inscription of 782 as &quot;the slayer of courageous enemies&quot;, took the Shailendras' power to Ligor on the Malay Peninsula and rose to be Maharaja of <b>Srivijaya</b>, by conquest or by kinship.</blockquote><a href="/empire/medang-kingdom">Medang Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kavirajamarga, a treatise on poetics attributed to Amoghavarsha I or his court poet <b>Srivijaya</b>, is the earliest surviving literary work in Kannada, and in the tenth century the poets Pampa, author of the Vikramarjuna Vijaya and a Jain Adipurana completed in 941, and Ponna worked under the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
